{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":2121,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,6]],"date-time":"2026-11-06T12:27:12Z","timestamp":1793968032503,"version":"4.8.0"},"reference-count":66,"publisher":"MDPI AG","issue":"11","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,6]],"date-time":"2026-11-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1793923200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,6]],"date-time":"2026-11-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1793923200000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Urban Sci.","Urban Science"],"abstract":"<jats:p>Riyadh\u2019s rapid urban growth has created challenges in balancing arterial mobility with pedestrian-scale public-space provision. This paper explores the humanization of urban arterial streets as a model shift in transforming them into more walkable, inclusive, and climate-responsive public spaces, based on four principles: human scale, walkability and active mobility, social interaction and placemaking, and contextual adaptation. Riyadh\u2019s Sports Boulevard, a flagship project under Saudi Vision 2030, is examined as the case study. The study combines a literature review, field observation of the completed corridor segment, and a two-tier comparative analysis. The first tier evaluates the Sports Boulevard and three precedents using observable indicators derived from the four principles, while the second reviews AI-enabled systems from three international cases to identify their humanization function and examine their potential relevance to Riyadh\u2019s hyper-arid context. The analysis shows that humanizing arterial streets depends not only on spatial redesign but also on sensing, simulation, and adaptive control. Findings show th